# Manuscript PDF page 64

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I sent the kid in Soundview the second half of his EP. No note. No
apology. No let me know what you think. Just the files, one tap, and a
thumb that didn’t shake until after it was gone.
   It might flop. I sent it anyway.
   Walking out, I passed the gate again. The guy in the gray jacket was
gone, but he’d left a stack of flyers on the bench with a rock on top. At
the far end of the path, a hundred feet off, a man was lighting a cigar‐
ette with his back to me and his cap pulled low.
   I knew the walk.
   I don’t know why I did it. Maybe because nobody had done it in two
years. I lifted two fingers to my left temple, the way we did when we
were kids, across the fourth-floor landing, every morning on the way to
school. Dos. The signal that meant I’m here, and so are you. I held it.
   He turned his head, fast. The cap hid his face. His shoulders went
up, startled by a stranger or recognized by a ghost, I couldn’t tell
which. He dropped the cigarette, half-lit, and walked away down the
path without looking back, left shoulder dipping, the other way.
   I kept two fingers at my temple until the path was empty.




